<LanguageData>

  <!-- traitAdjectives are GENDER-INVARIANT throughout, and that is a hard requirement rather than a
       style choice: the namer postposes them onto a [weapon_noun] whose gender it cannot know, and
       this mod's roster is four feminine weapons (hache, épée longue, masse, lance) against three
       masculine ones (glaive, couteau, marteau de guerre). Odyssey's own French trait adjectives break
       this constantly (léger, légère, lourde, perçante, laid), which mostly survives on its almost
       entirely masculine gun roster and would be wrong more often than right here. Two shapes are
       therefore used, both attested in Odyssey's French file: a prepositional phrase (à..., de...,
       en..., sans..., au...) or an adjective already invariant in gender (one whose masculine form
       ends in -e: agile, féroce, magnifique, malcommode, sans pitié).

       Each phrase also stays material-neutral: a universal trait rolls on wood and jade as well as
       steel, so the carbonized adjective names the surface, not a substrate.

       The six Odyssey ports (Cumbersome, GoldInlay, JadeInlay, Lightweight, Ornamental, Ugly) take
       Odyssey's official French labels, and its descriptions verbatim wherever our English matches it
       word for word; Lightweight and Cumbersome differ only in aim-vs-swing, so just that clause is
       adapted. As in the other languages, Odyssey's Ugly adjective indices differ from ours, so they
       are re-mapped by meaning. The "Traders will pay..." sentences are Odyssey's verbatim. -->

